JWT (JSON Web Token)
What it is
A self-contained token that holds user data (claims) inside it. No server-side storage needed. It's a string with 3 parts:
HEADER.PAYLOAD.SIGNATURE
eyJhbGci...  .  eyJ1c2VySWQi...  .  abc123sig...

Header → algorithm used (HS256, RS256)
Payload → actual data (userId, role, exp)
Signature → HMAC of header+payload with a secret key

The server verifies the signature to trust the token. It never needs to "look it up."

JWT Flow
1. User logs in → POST /login { email, password }
2. Server validates credentials
3. Server creates JWT → signs it with SECRET_KEY
4. Server sends JWT to client
5. Client stores it (localStorage / cookie)

--- every request after ---

6. Client sends:  Authorization: Bearer <token>
7. Server receives token
8. Server VERIFIES signature (no DB call needed)
9. Decodes payload → gets userId, role, etc.
10. Grants or denies access


SESSION (Server-Side Sessions)
What it is
The server stores user data on the backend (memory, DB, Redis). The client only holds a session ID (a random opaque string like sess:abc123). On each request, the server uses that ID to look up the real data.

Session Flow
1. User logs in → POST /login { email, password }
2. Server validates credentials
3. Server creates a session object → stores in memory/Redis
   { sessionId: "abc123", userId: 42, role: "admin" }
4. Server sends back a cookie:  Set-Cookie: sessionId=abc123

--- every request after ---

5. Browser auto-sends cookie:  Cookie: sessionId=abc123
6. Server receives sessionId
7. Server looks up "abc123" in store → finds user data
8. Grants or denies access


