Redis — Its Relation to Both
Redis + Sessions (Primary use case)
Sessions need a fast shared store — this is where Redis shines.
Problem without Redis:
  - App runs on 3 servers
  - User logs in on Server 1 → session stored in Server 1 memory
  - Next request hits Server 2 → session not found → logged out ❌

Solution with Redis:
  - All servers connect to ONE Redis instance
  - Session stored in Redis, not in-process memory
  - Any server can look up any session ✅
sessionId: "abc123"  →  Redis key: "sess:abc123"
                          Redis val: { userId: 42, role: "admin" }
                          TTL: 3600s (auto-expires)
Redis is perfect for this because:

Sub-millisecond reads
Built-in TTL (sessions auto-expire)
Shared across all instances


Redis + JWT (Blacklisting / Revocation)
JWT's biggest weakness is you can't revoke it before expiry. Redis solves this:
Logout flow with Redis blacklist:
1. User logs out
2. Server adds token's jti (JWT ID) to Redis:
   SET blacklist:jti_abc123  "1"  EX 3600
3. On every request, server checks:
   EXISTS blacklist:jti_abc123 → if yes, reject ❌

Result: You can now "invalidate" JWTs ✅
This gives JWT the revocation power of sessions, at the cost of one Redis lookup per request.

When to Use What
Use JWT when:
  ✅ Microservices / distributed systems
  ✅ Mobile apps (no cookies)
  ✅ Stateless APIs
  ✅ Short-lived tokens (15min access + refresh token)

Use Sessions when:
  ✅ Traditional web apps
  ✅ You need instant logout/revoke
  ✅ Sensitive user data (don't expose in token)
  ✅ Fine-grained session control

Use Redis when:
  ✅ Scaling sessions across multiple servers
  ✅ JWT blacklisting
  ✅ Both patterns at production scale

The Production Pattern (Best of both worlds)
Most production systems do this:
Access Token  → JWT, short-lived (15 min)       → stateless, fast
Refresh Token → stored in Redis/DB, long-lived  → revocable, secure
Short-lived JWTs limit damage if stolen. Refresh tokens in Redis give you full revocation control.
